data: {"type": "assistant_msg", "ts": "2026-08-06T12:27:08.058269+00:00", "content": "Based on the comprehensive telemetry evidence gathered, **there is strong evidence that ce_branch24's faults were caused by a P-router issue (specifically in the MPLS underlay path) rather than the CE itself.** Here's the analysis:\n\n## Key Evidence\n\n### 1. **ce_branch24 shows extreme tunnel impairment across ALL three hubs simultaneously**\n- **Jitter spikes**: All three tunnels (ce_hub1, ce_hub2, ce_hub3) show correlated massive jitter spikes (up to 77ms, 69ms, 71ms respectively) at the same timestamps [metrics:0], [metrics:21], [metrics:40]\n- **Latency spikes**: All three tunnels show correlated latency spikes (100-105ms) simultaneously [metrics:0], [metrics:21], [metrics:41]\n- **Loss spikes**: All three tunnels show extreme loss spikes (27-50% loss) at correlated times \u2014 e.g., 39-50% loss at ts=1786018708/1786018738 on hub2/hub3 [metrics:28], [metrics:29], [metrics:46], [metrics:47]\n\n**A single CE device cannot independently cause identical impairment patterns across three diverse hub paths unless the common underlay is failing.**\n\n### 2. **The upstream P-routers (p23, p24) and PE (pe12) show STABLE MPLS/OSPF metrics**\n- **MPLS LSP counts**: p23 (108), p24 (108), pe12 (113) \u2014 all rock-stable across the entire window [topo:p23], [topo:p24], [topo:pe12] and confirmed in ranged queries\n- **LDP sessions**: pe12's LDP sessions to p23/p24 (10.255.1.23/.24) remain in state=5 (operational) throughout [metrics:0-39]\n- **OSPF neighbors**: p23's OSPF neighbors (10.255.1.21/.22) stay in state=1 (Full) throughout [metrics:20-49]\n- **No routing churn**: OSPF LSA counts stable at 204 on p23/p24, 396 on p21/p22 [topo:p23], [topo:p21]\n\n### 3. **Neighbor CE (ce_branch12) on same PE (pe12) shows NORMAL tunnel health**\n- **ce_branch12 jitter**: Consistently low (1-9ms) across all hubs [metrics:0-49]\n- **ce_branch12 loss**: Generally 0.03-3.5% (mostly <1%) \u2014 **orders of magnitude better** than ce_branch24's 27-50% spikes [metrics:0-49]\n- **ce_branch12 latency**: Stable 64-92ms vs ce_branch24's 70-105ms spikes\n\nThis is the **smoking gun**: ce_branch12 shares the same PE (pe12) and upstream P-routers (p23/p24) but experiences **none** of the massive impairment ce_branch24 sees.\n\n### 4. **ce_branch24 hardware is healthy**\n- Temperature 28.7\u00b0C, fan 3000 RPM, PSU 11.8V, CPU 0.13%, memory 0.19% [topo:ce_branch24]\n- Interface operational, minimal discards/errors [topo:ce_branch24]\n- Optical Rx -5.98 dBm (good), Tx bias 28.2mA (normal) [topo:ce_branch24]\n\n### 5. **Topology confirms the path**\nce_branch24 \u2192 pe12 \u2192 p23/p24 (core) \u2192 hubs. The fact that **ce_branch12 on the same pe12\u2192p23/p24 path is healthy** while **ce_branch24 on that same path is severely impaired** means the fault is **specific to ce_branch24's path segment** \u2014 likely the **ce_branch24\u2194pe12 access link or pe12's interface toward ce_branch24**.\n\n## Conclusion\nThe evidence **excludes a P-router/core issue** (p23/p24/pe12 MPLS/OSPF are stable, and ce_branch12 on the same path is healthy) and **excludes a CE hardware issue** (ce_branch24 hardware is normal). The fault is localized to **ce_branch24's specific access path to pe12** \u2014 likely a **Layer 1/2 issue on the ce_branch24\u2194pe12 link** (fiber, optics, cross-connect, or pe12's interface config for that circuit)."}

